  {"type": "split", "photo": "sys", "h2": "Sizing a heat pump for the lower city versus the Mountain",
   "html": "<p>Hamilton is two climates with one postal prefix. Below the escarpment, lake air keeps winters a touch milder and summers heavier. Up top, Hamilton Mountain, Ancaster and Binbrook sit higher, catch more wind and hold snow longer. The same 1,800 square foot house can have a noticeably different heat loss depending on which side of the Jolley Cut it sits on.</p>"
           "<p>That is why we don't size from square footage. We run a Manual J calculation that accounts for wall construction, window area, air leakage and the design temperature for your part of the city. From that we pick the tonnage and find the balance point, the outdoor temperature where the heat pump's output equals the house's loss. For most Hamilton homes a cold-climate unit carries the load well below -15°C on its own.</p>"
           "<p>Below the balance point, backup takes over: the existing boiler in a radiator home, a gas furnace in a <a href='/heat-pump/hybrid-furnace-systems/'>hybrid system</a>, or electric strips in an air handler. We show you where that point lands for your house, so you know what the hydro and gas bills will look like, not just the purchase price.</p>"
           "<ul class=\"checklist\"><li>Design load from Manual J, not a rule of thumb</li>"
           "<li>Capacity checked at -15°C, not just the nameplate rating</li>"
           "<li>Backup sized to the gap, not to the whole house</li>"
           "<li>Duct static pressure measured before any ducted unit is quoted</li></ul>"},
